<TopicPage slug="subjects/operations-research/foundational/linear-programming">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Linear programming (LP) is the workhorse of operations research: maximise or minimise a
    linear objective — profit, cost, distance — subject to linear constraints. An unreasonable
    share of the world's logistics, manufacturing, scheduling, blending and crew planning runs
    on it. Why linear? Partly because so many real trade-offs are proportional (twice the
    output takes twice the labour), but mostly because linear models <em>solve</em>: not by
    guesswork, but by a geometric theory that guarantees the optimum can be found, and an
    algorithm — the simplex method, George Dantzig, 1947 — that turns "solve" into a walk
    between corners of a shape. Problems with thousands of variables and constraints are solved
    to provable optimality in seconds on a laptop, which is why LP became the first tool OR
    teaches rather than one among many.
  </p>
  <p>
    The intellectual arc of the field fits in one sentence: <em>formulation</em> turns a messy
    decision into algebra; <em>geometry</em> shows the algebra is a shape whose corners hold
    the answers; <em>the simplex method</em> walks the corners efficiently; and
    <em>duality</em> reveals that every optimisation secretly contains a second problem — a
    price list — whose solution says what each bottleneck is worth. This page builds that arc
    with one running example, then looks at where LP ends and its harder relatives begin.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="variables, objective, constraints, vertex, duality" widget="equationStep" config={lpCfg} />

  <h2>Formulation: turning a decision into algebra</h2>
  <p>
    Every LP is assembled from the same four parts. <strong>Decision variables</strong>: the
    quantities you may choose — x<sub>1</sub> tables and x<sub>2</sub> chairs per week in our
    running example. <strong>An objective function</strong>: a linear expression to maximise
    or minimise — say profit of 30 per table and 20 per chair, so maximise
    30x<sub>1</sub> + 20x<sub>2</sub>. <strong>Constraints</strong>: linear inequalities
    capturing limited resources — carpentry allows 40 hours weekly (2 hours per table, 1 per
    chair: 2x<sub>1</sub> + x<sub>2</sub> ≤ 40), finishing allows 30 hours (x<sub>1</sub> +
    x<sub>2</sub> ≤ 30) — plus <strong>non-negativity</strong> (x<sub>1</sub>, x<sub>2</sub> ≥ 0;
    you cannot make negative tables). The translation is the analyst's real craft, because it
    is where modelling errors enter: variables must be defined with units and time frames
    (tables <em>per week</em>), constraints must capture genuinely binding limits, and
    everything the objective or constraints touch must behave <em>proportionally</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    That last requirement — proportionality — is what makes a model linear, and LP courses
    spend real time on its boundary. The standard assumptions: <strong>proportionality</strong>
    (contributions scale with the variable, no setup costs or quantity discounts),
    <strong>additivity</strong> (effects of variables simply sum, no interaction terms),
    <strong>divisibility</strong> (fractional answers make sense — true for blending tonnes of
    ore, false for "build one more aircraft", which needs integer programming),
    <strong>certainty</strong> (the coefficients are known numbers, not gambles — relaxed by
    stochastic programming). Formulation is also an act of honest scoping: the modeller decides
    what the variables are allowed to mean, and a well-posed LP is often worth as much as its
    solution because it makes the trade-offs <em>explicit</em> — the meetings it disciplines
    are part of its value.
  </p>

  <h2>Geometry and the simplex algorithm: corners, then a walk</h2>
  <p>
    In two variables the whole theory is visible, which is why it is taught drawn on paper.
    Each constraint is a line that cuts the plane in half (a <strong>half-space</strong>);
    stacking all the halves leaves the <strong>feasible region</strong> — a polygon (in higher
    dimensions, a polyhedron) containing exactly the choices that obey every constraint. The
    objective is a family of parallel lines — every point on one line earns the same profit,
    so the lines are <em>iso-profit</em> lines. Maximising means sliding the iso-profit line
    outward as far as it can go while still touching the feasible region; the plot below shows
    the moment of last contact, and it is a theorem, not a coincidence: <strong>the optimum of
    a linear program lies at a vertex</strong> (extreme point) of the feasible region. If the
    objective were parallel to a constraint, a whole edge ties for best — and a vertex of that
    edge still wins. This <em>corner-point theorem</em> is the licence for everything that
    follows: instead of searching infinitely many points, search finitely many corners.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="constraints, feasible region and the optimal vertex" widget="plotter" config={feasibleCfg} />

  <p>
    Finishing the running example by hand makes the theorem concrete. The feasible region's
    vertices are the origin; (20, 0), where carpentry alone binds; (0, 30), where finishing
    alone binds; and the intersection of both constraints — solving 2x<sub>1</sub> + x<sub>2</sub>
    = 40 with x<sub>1</sub> + x<sub>2</sub> = 30 gives x<sub>1</sub> = 10, x<sub>2</sub> = 20.
    Evaluating profit at each: 0; 600; 600; and 30(10) + 20(20) = <strong>700 at (10, 20)</strong>
    — ten tables and twenty chairs, both resources fully used. With two variables, evaluating
    four corners is trivial; with two thousand variables and as many constraints, the vertex
    count is combinatorial, and evaluating them all is hopeless. The <strong>simplex
    method</strong> is the escape: start at any vertex, look along the edges leaving it, move
    along whichever edge improves the objective fastest, and repeat. At each step the profit
    rises; when no leaving edge improves it, the current vertex is optimal — the corner-point
    theorem guarantees that stopping rule is correct. Algebraically, the method adds
    <strong>slack variables</strong> (measuring unused capacity of each resource) so the
    inequalities become equations, then "pivots" one variable into the basis and another out —
    bookkeeping that a computer performs at industrial scale. Two honest caveats belong in a
    first course: simplex is spectacular in practice yet has exponential worst cases (the
    Klee–Minty cube), and the <strong>interior-point methods</strong> introduced by Karmarkar
    in 1984 — walking <em>through</em> the polytope rather than around its edges — solved
    provably in polynomial time and now share the heavy lifting with simplex in commercial
    solvers. Neither caveat changes the geometry; both explain why the 1980s doubled the size
    of solvable problems overnight.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that LP is "linear algebra with inequalities" — a mechanical exercise. Formulation is modelling judgement (choosing variables, spotting binding resources, refusing linearity where it does not hold), and duality is economic interpretation, not algebra hygiene. The mathematics is the easy part; the professional skill is making the algebra true of the world.</p>

  <h2>Duality and sensitivity: every plan has a price list</h2>
  <p>
    LP's deepest idea is that every maximisation problem (the <strong>primal</strong>) is
    shadowed by a minimisation problem (the <strong>dual</strong>) built from the same data:
    one dual variable per constraint, interpreting naturally as a <em>price</em>. For the
    furniture example the dual asks: what should carpentry hours and finishing hours be worth,
    per hour, so that no product is more profitable to make than its inputs justify, while
    minimising the total imputed value of the capacity? Solving both yields
    <strong>strong duality</strong>: the optimal objective values coincide — the best plan and
    the best price list meet exactly. The dual variables at the optimum are the
    <strong>shadow prices</strong>: for our example both come out at 10 — one more carpentry
    hour (within range) adds 10 to weekly profit, as does one more finishing hour. Every
    binding constraint gets a positive shadow price; non-binding ones get zero (unused
    capacity is worth nothing — the free lunch the model refuses to serve).
  </p>
  <p>
    This turns a solution into a negotiation tool, and sensitivity analysis extends it. The
    <strong>shadow price</strong> says what a resource is worth at the margin: bidding for
    overtime below 10 per hour raises profit; above it, loses money. Airlines run the same
    logic on gates and landing slots; refineries on crude qualities; streaming logistics on
    warehouse capacity. <strong>Reduced costs</strong> price the variables not in the plan —
    a third product whose profit would need to rise by 4 per unit before it earns a place in
    the mix. And <strong>ranging</strong> asks how far a coefficient can move before the
    current basis stops being optimal, defining the validity limits of every shadow price.
    Complementary slackness ties it all in a sentence: a resource with slack has zero price,
    and a product with positive price must be produced — the algebraic echo of market
    clearing, which is why duality, discovered by von Neumann in 1947 (and independently
    embedded in Kantorovich's Soviet planning work, recognised by the 1975 Nobel prize), is
    taught in economics departments as often as in engineering ones.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    A second worked example shows why structure matters as much as size. The
    <strong>transportation problem</strong> — ship from m factories to n warehouses at the
    least total cost, with each factory's supply and each warehouse's demand fixed — is an
    LP, but its constraint matrix is so sparse and regular (each variable appears in exactly
    two constraints: its origin and its destination) that specialised structure takes over.
    The matrix is <em>totally unimodular</em>, which guarantees that the LP's optimal vertex
    is automatically integral: no fractional shipments appear, no integer programming is
    needed, and the simplex method specialises into the network simplex, which walks the
    spanning trees of the underlying graph and solves problems with millions of arcs.
    Assignment, shortest path and maximum flow inherit the same property. The general
    lesson, and the reason OR courses teach transportation separately, is that recognising
    hidden structure routinely outperforms raw solver power — a special case that took
    minutes as a general LP becomes milliseconds as a network.
  </p>
  <p>
    The LP rabbit hole goes far. <strong>Structure exploits</strong>: the field's classical
    applications — transportation, assignment, shortest path, max flow — are LPs with such
    special structure that their constraint matrices are totally unimodular, guaranteeing
    integer solutions for free; network simplex exploits the graph to run faster still. The
    general lesson: before reaching for general-purpose machinery, check whether your problem
    is a known special case wearing a costume. <strong>LP as a subroutine</strong>: integer
    programming — the "build or not" world of facilities, capital budgeting and scheduling —
    is NP-hard, but its standard solution machinery (branch and bound, branch and cut) runs
    on the <em>LP relaxation</em> at every node, so LP quality directly determines integer
    solve times; decomposition methods (Dantzig–Wolfe, Benders) split enormous LPs into
    manageable pieces by exploiting the dual. <strong>Convexity's promise</strong>: LP is the
    sharpest special case of convex optimisation — local optimality implies global, duality
    is clean, and the same geometry (half-spaces, polytopes) generalises to quadratic and
    conic programs used in portfolio optimisation and control. And the frontier is practical
    rather than theoretical: modern solvers (CPLEX, Gurobi, and strong open-source cousins)
    preprocess, presolve and cut aggressively, so a first-course model that took 1960s
    computers minutes now takes microseconds — which is why the durable skills of this unit
    are formulation discipline, geometric intuition, and the habit of asking not just "what
    is the optimal plan?" but "what is the plan's price list, and within what ranges can I
    trust it?" Those three questions, asked in that order, are linear programming.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> MIT OR curriculum (OCW); Dantzig, <em>Linear Programming and Extensions</em>; Bertsimas &amp; Tsitsiklis, <em>Introduction to Linear Optimization</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
